---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import ModelWorkbench from '../../../components/tutorials/ModelWorkbench';
import Math from '../../../components/common/Math.astro';
import MathBlock from '../../../components/common/MathBlock.astro';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Mass-Spring-Damper"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/tutorials/getting-started' },
    { label: 'Mass-Spring-Damper' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Your First Model', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/first-model' }}
  next={{ label: 'Bouncing Ball', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/bouncing-ball' }}
>
  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded-lg border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent mb-2">What you'll learn</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-sm text-text-muted space-y-1">
      <li>How parameters control physical behavior (stiffness, damping, mass)</li>
      <li>Damped oscillations and how energy dissipates over time</li>
      <li>How to read a second-order ODE system in Modelica</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The mass-spring-damper is the canonical introductory ODE system. A mass <em>m</em> is attached to a spring
    with stiffness <em>k</em> and a damper with coefficient <em>c</em>. The governing equations are:
  </p>
  <MathBlock tex={`\\begin{aligned}
\\frac{dx}{dt} &= v \\\\
m\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} &= -k\\,x - c\\,v
\\end{aligned}`} />
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    In Modelica, this is expressed naturally. Simulate the model below and watch the position oscillate
    and decay as the damper removes energy from the system.
  </p>

  <ModelWorkbench
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model MassSpringDamper
  Real x(start = 1);
  Real v(start = 0);
  parameter Real k = 1, c = 0.1, m = 1;
equation
  der(x) = v;
  m * der(v) = -k * x - c * v;
end MassSpringDamper;`}
    modelName="MassSpringDamper"
    tEnd={10}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">Understanding the results</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    With the default parameters, the system is <strong class="text-text">underdamped</strong> &mdash; position
    oscillates while the amplitude decays exponentially. The damper converts kinetic energy into heat, so
    each swing is smaller than the last. Toggle <em>v</em> in the plot legend to see the velocity
    lagging position by roughly a quarter cycle, a hallmark of oscillatory systems.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Things to try</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2">
    <li>Set <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">c = 0</code> to remove damping entirely &mdash; the system oscillates forever.</li>
    <li>Increase <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">c</code> to <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">3.0</code> to see <strong class="text-text">overdamped</strong> behavior (no oscillation, slow return to zero).</li>
    <li>Find the <strong class="text-text">critically damped</strong> value: <Math tex={`c = 2\\sqrt{km}`} /> = 2.0 with the defaults. This is the fastest return to zero without overshooting.</li>
    <li>Double the spring stiffness <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">k</code> &mdash; the oscillation frequency increases.</li>
  </ul>
</TutorialLayout>
